            <ArticleTitle>Enhanced frontal cortex activation in rats by convergent amygdaloid and noxious sensory signals.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>2395-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The modulation of frontal cortical EEG activation to noxious somatosensory (tail pressure) and olfactory (acetone) stimulation by the basal amygdala was examined in urethane-anesthetized rats. Mild tail pressure produced no EEG activation, while acetone (sniffed by freely breathing rats or drawn across the olfactory epithelium in tracheotomized rats) produced a moderate suppression of large-amplitude synchronized EEG patterns. Concurrent, low-intensity 100 Hz stimulation of the basal amygdala permitted EEG activation to tail pressure to occur, and strongly enhanced olfactory-induced cortical activation. These results indicate that excitation of the basal amygdala potentiates frontal cortical responsiveness to aversive sensory events. This may provide a mechanism to facilitate cortical excitability and processing by amygdaloid neuronal activity.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
